Hecks.hecksagon "Inbox" do
  # Inbox wiring. The Gmail poll's side effect is no longer an
  # imperative `:exec` adapter resolved by a hand-written Rust arm
  # (resolve_exec_adapters, retired) — it is ordinary bluebook now.
  # See inbox.bluebook's `RunPollOnInboxChecked` policy : when
  # Inbox.Check emits InboxChecked, the aggregate-qualified policy
  # (`on "Inbox.InboxChecked"`, gap #1b) fires the generic
  # process-spawn primitive `Primitive::Process.Spawn` (literal cmd
  # `node bin/inbox_poll.mjs`, result_into `Cascade.RecordResult`).
  # The runtime's one generic primitive hook runs the poller and
  # cascades the outcome — the adapters-as-bluebook arc (plan:
  # structured-pondering-pie, gap #1b slice).
  #
  # The Inbox bluebook (sibling inbox.bluebook) locks Check to a
  # multi-step poll : OAuth refresh, Gmail historyId delta since the
  # watermark, known-correspondent filter, per new thread a card
  # write + a drafts-only acknowledgement (never starting the work).
  # Pure composition — the bluebook names the contract. The script
  # bin/inbox_poll.mjs is the impure leaf the primitive runs.
  #
  # This closes i629 : the dispatch IS the fetch. When the overmind
  # `inbox` loop dispatches Inbox::Inbox.Check every 900s — OR Miette
  # dispatches it on demand via the storehouse universal door — the
  # poll runs and cascades its outcome, joinable by id. "No new
  # process" (Chris's locked decision) stays literally true.
  #
  # cwd contract : the process-spawn primitive (exec_dispatcher leaf)
  # inherits the runtime process cwd. The overmind `inbox` member runs
  # from hecks_conception/, so `bin/inbox_poll.mjs` resolves identically
  # whether the loop or a manual dispatch fired it.

  adapter :memory
end
